		"5": {
			"name": "The Impossible Link",
			"goal_text": "Make a Bio-Mite that is Square AND Neon, or press It's impossible if it can't be done.",
			"goal_phenotype": {
				"Body Shape": "Square",
				"Glow": "Neon"
			},
			"hint": "Square is recessive, so a Square Bio-Mite needs two Square chromosomes. Check what else every Square chromosome carries.",
			"focus": "The Aha! Moment: Student must conclude that without breaking the chromosome, this combination is impossible.",
			"is_impossible": true,
			"impossible_explanation": "Body Shape and Glow are on the same chromosome: they are linked. Square is recessive, so a Square Bio-Mite needs two Square chromosomes, and every Square chromosome here also carries Dull. So both copies are Dull, and these chromosomes can't make a Square, Neon Bio-Mite.\n\nIn real living things, crossing over can break this kind of link. When egg and sperm cells form, paired chromosomes can swap matching pieces, which can put Square and Neon on one chromosome. Genes far apart on a chromosome get separated this way more often than genes close together. This game doesn't model crossing over: its chromosomes always stay whole.",
